WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:06.320
Multiple alerts show account takeovers, rapid orders, and refund attempts using related infrastructure.

00:06.640 --> 00:09.120
The pattern suggests an active incident.

00:09.440 --> 00:15.200
The first priority is to recognize the scope quickly and declare the incident through the established escalation path.

00:15.600 --> 00:19.120
Contain the affected path without making uncontrolled changes.

00:19.520 --> 00:26.400
Pause risky transactions or fulfillment, disable exposed credentials, restrict compromised tools or destinations,

00:26.680 --> 00:28.320
and protect customer accounts.

00:28.560 --> 00:33.120
Use the narrowest control that reduces harm while preserving legitimate activity.

00:33.440 --> 00:36.240
Escalate to the teams named in the response plan.

00:36.560 --> 00:42.640
Fraud, security, payments, customer service, legal, and leadership may each have a role.

00:43.040 --> 00:49.200
Assign one incident lead, decision owners, a communication channel, and a regular update schedule.

00:49.520 --> 00:54.080
Preserve evidence before systems, records, or attacker behavior change.

00:54.480 --> 01:03.840
Capture alerts, logs, authentication events, transactions, account histories, support contacts, and the versions of rules or models in use.

01:04.239 --> 01:09.200
Record when evidence was collected, by whom, and any action that altered the environment.

01:09.520 --> 01:12.880
Assess impact using confirmed and possible categories.

01:13.200 --> 01:21.200
Identify affected customers, accounts, orders, funds, products, regions, systems, and time periods.

01:21.520 --> 01:26.960
Distinguish what the team knows from what it is still investigating, and update the scope as evidence changes.

01:27.280 --> 01:30.000
Begin recovery only when controls are ready.

01:30.320 --> 01:37.680
Reset credentials, protect customers, attempt payment recovery, test fixes, and restore services in stages.

01:38.000 --> 01:43.120
Monitor for attacker adaptation and for unintended harm caused by emergency controls.

01:43.440 --> 01:49.520
Throughout the incident, document decisions, owners, timestamps, evidence, and results.

01:49.920 --> 01:57.280
A disciplined record supports recovery, customer communication, dispute handling, and the lessons that strengthen the next response.
